<p>
  Summer ambient temperatures in the Gulf and the Sahel routinely climb past what many instruments are designed for, and dust and sand find their way into everything. A meter that behaves in an air-conditioned office can misread or fail on a rooftop or in a switchyard. This guide covers how to protect measurement accuracy and instrument life in harsh field conditions.
</p>

<h2 id="read-the-limits">Read the Operating and Storage Limits</h2>

<p>
  Every instrument has an operating temperature range and a wider storage range, and accuracy is usually only guaranteed inside the operating one. Check both before taking an instrument to a hot site. Outside the range, displays can slow or blacken, batteries lose capacity and readings drift. If your sites regularly exceed the stated range, choose a different instrument rather than hoping for the best.
</p>

<h2 id="vehicle-heat">Never Leave It in the Vehicle</h2>

<p>
  A parked vehicle in the sun can reach temperatures far above ambient. Instruments left inside suffer battery swelling, display damage and slow degradation of components. Carry the kit with you or store it in a shaded, ventilated place. Keep the instrument in its case when not in use.
</p>

<h2 id="battery-heat">Batteries and Heat</h2>

<p>
  Heat shortens battery life and can damage rechargeable cells that are charged while hot. Let an instrument cool to a moderate temperature before charging. Carry spare cells, and in very remote work consider hand-driven instruments, which need no battery at all. CIE's hand-driven <a href={u('/products/insulation-testers-hand/')}>insulation testers</a> and <a href={u('/products/earth-tester/')}>earth testers</a> are built around a hand generator for this reason.
</p>

<h2 id="dust-sand">Dust, Sand and Ingress</h2>

<p>
  Fine dust works into switches, sockets and leads, and conductive dust can affect high-resistance readings. Close covers and cases between uses, wipe the instrument and leads clean, and avoid changing batteries in windy, dusty conditions. Check the <a href={u('/blog/ip-rating-explained/')}>IP rating</a> if you need protection against dust and water, and note that many general-purpose meters have no formal ingress rating.
</p>

<h2 id="condensation">Condensation When Moving Between Environments</h2>

<p>
  Taking a cold instrument from an air-conditioned vehicle into hot humid air causes condensation inside the case and on the circuit board. Let it acclimatise in its case for some time before use, and never power up a visibly misted instrument. Condensation is a common hidden cause of odd readings, particularly on high-resistance ranges. See <a href={u('/blog/insulation-testing-hot-humid-coastal-climates/')}>insulation testing in hot, humid and coastal climates</a> for how this affects megohm readings.
</p>

<h2 id="thermal-effects">Thermal Effects on the Measurement Itself</h2>

<p>
  Hot leads and connections change resistance, and temperature affects the thing you are measuring too. Low-resistance measurements in particular are sensitive to lead and contact condition, which is why four-terminal measurement is used for precision work. Read about <a href={u('/blog/micro-ohm-meter-guide/')}>micro-ohm meters</a> and <a href={u('/blog/insulation-resistance-temperature-correction/')}>temperature correction of insulation readings</a>.
</p>

<h2 id="recalibration">Harsh Use Means Shorter Calibration Intervals</h2>

<p>
  An instrument used in heat, dust and vibration drifts faster than one on a lab shelf. Shorten the calibration interval for field instruments and check them against a known reference between formal calibrations. The <a href={u('/blog/calibration-interval-guide/')}>calibration interval guide</a> explains how to set the schedule.
</p>

<Callout type="tip" title="Quick field-care checklist">
  Keep it shaded, cased and clean, let it acclimatise, carry spare batteries, and verify against a reference before important measurements.
</Callout>

<p>
  Need an instrument for demanding conditions? <a href={u('/contact/')}>Describe your site</a> and we will recommend options.
</p>
